<p>If trainers get caught up in a fight with wild Pokémon they do not think they can win against, they can attempt to <dfn>flee</dfn>. Players may flee in a number of creative ways, such as by using a move to hinder the opponent, using food to distract the pokémon, or simply running as fast as they can. Making a Group Check (Dungeon Master's Guide, page 28) or running a Chase (Dungeon Master's Guide, page 52) are a good ways to resolve the success of the group. But beware! Fleeing may only be attempted by the group once per round of combat.</p>
<p>Note that some moves, items, or abilities may interact with a party's attempt to flee. For example, a wild Pokémon with <a href="{Url.moves("pursuit")}">Pursuit</a> may react with an attack before the party escapes.</p>

<Heading level="3" id="fleeing-example">Example</Heading>
<p>The party is overwhelmed by an Ursaring. Oops! They decide that their best way to escape is by distracting it with a few Sitrus Berries they have.</p>
<p>The DM decides to ask for a group Deception check, made with advantage because Ursarings love berries. Each player in the party makes a Deception check DC 15, with 3 passing and 1 failing. Since half or more of the party succeeded, the Ursaring starts gathering the thrown berries and the party is able to escape.</p>